y  agree on something they reach a  consensus so the agreement here is the  order and content of which operations  have happened in the entire history of  that tablet so when we insert a bunch of  rows one of the the server's is going to  act as a leader and sort of sequence  them and say this is the order that they  got inserted in here are the rows and  then run this algorithm to ensure that  everybody agrees yeah these rows were  inserted in this order when that  algorithm is complete we know that the  three replicas are completely  synchronized and if one of them crashes  we can just failover immediately to one  of the other ones so typical failover  times are less than five seconds you  could probably tune down your failover  times if you really need it faster  failover but this is pretty sufficient  for most applications and because we're  running the replication up at our level  in this kind of database log we actually  don't need a distributed file system  underneath so there's no dependency of  kudou on any other storage system you  can just deploy kudou on some nodes  you're good to go  you don't need HDFS you don't need  zookeeper everything is built-in so the  other process in addition to the tablet  servers is the master so the master is a  metadata server for the cluster you can  think of it almost like a phone book or  a directory so it does a couple simple  things the first keeping track of which  tablets exist once they split up a table  into tablets we need to know where they  are  the second is which tables exist their  schemas their metadata information who  created them that kind of thing and the  third is policy decisions so load  balancing if the server crashes we need  to make a new replica somewhere  Cattleya pick where well it's easiest to  centralize the policy if you put it in a  single place like the master you can be  a lot smarter about these policies but  notice it doesn't say anything about  data here it's only metadata so no data  e